REGIONAL REGULATION OF TRENGGALEK REGENCY

NUMBER 10 OF 2012

CONCERNING

IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR WOMEN AND CHILDREN

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

THE REGENT OF TRENGGALEK,

Considering:

a. that the Unitary State of the Republic of Indonesia guarantees the welfare of each of its citizens, including protection of women's rights and children's rights which are human rights;
b. that in order to improve the position, role, and quality of women is an effort to realize gender equality and justice in family, community, national, and state life;
c. that every child is a bud, potential, and the next generation of the nation's struggle, and is expected to be able to bear that responsibility in the future, therefore they need to be given the widest possible opportunity to grow and develop optimally, both physically, mentally and socially, and have noble character, so protection efforts need to be carried out as well as to realize child welfare by providing guarantees for the fulfillment of their rights and treatment without discrimination;
d. that violence against women and children is a crime against humanity which constitutes a violation of human rights;
e. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, letter c, and letter d, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the Implementation of Protection for Women and Children;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 12 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Areas within the Province of East Java (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1950 Number 41, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 90) as amended by Law Number 2 of 1965 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1965 Number 19, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 2730);
3. Law Number 4 of 1979 concerning Child Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1979 Number 32, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3143);
4. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on The Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
5. Law Number 3 of 1997 concerning Juvenile Court (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1979 Number 3, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3668);
6. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
7. Law Number 1 of 2000 concerning Ratification of Convention Number 182 Concerning The Prohibition And Immediate Action for The Elimination of the Worst Forms of Child Labour (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2000 Number 30, Supplement to the State Gazette Number 3941);
8. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235);
9. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
10. Law Number 32 of 2004 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 125, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4437) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 12 of 2008 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2008 Number 59, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4844);
11. Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635);
12. Law of the Republic of Indonesia Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Crime of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
13. Law Number 11 of 2009 concerning Social Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 12, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4967);
14. Law Number 36 of 2009 concerning Health (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 144, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5063);
15. Law Number 12 of 2011 concerning the Formulation of Legislation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5234);
16. Law Number 19 of 2011 concerning Ratification of the Convention On The Rights Of Persons With Disabilities (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 107, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5251);
17. Government Regulation Number 58 of 2005 concerning Regional Financial Management (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2005 Number 140, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4578);
18. Government Regulation Number 79 of 2005 concerning Guidelines for Guidance and Supervision of Regional Government Administration (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2005 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4593);
19. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning Implementation and Cooperation for Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
20. Government Regulation Number 38 of 2007 concerning Division of Government Affairs Between the Government, Provincial Regional Government, and Regency/City Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4737);
21. Government Regulation Number 44 of 2008 concerning Provision of Compensation, Restitution, and Assistance to Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2008 Number 84, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4860);
22. Presidential Decree Number 36 of 1990 Concerning Ratification of the Convention on The Rights of The Child;
23. Presidential Decree Number 23 of 2011 concerning the National Action Plan for Human Rights of Indonesia 2011-2014;
24. Joint Decree of 3 (three) Ministers and the Chief of Police Number: 14/MenegPemberdayaan Perempuan/Dep.V/X/2002 Number 1329/MENKES/SKB/X/2002, Number 75/HUK/2002, Number POL.B/3048/2002, concerning Integrated Services for Victims of Violence against Women and Children;
25. Presidential Decree Number 59 of 2002 concerning the National Action Plan for the Elimination of the Worst Forms of Child Labour;
26. Presidential Decree Number 52 of 2004 National Commission for the Elderly;
27. Presidential Regulation Number 65 of 2005 concerning the National Commission on Violence Against Women;
28. Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 13 of 2006 concerning Guidelines for Regional Financial Management as amended several times most recently by Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 21 of 2011 (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 310);
29. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 1 of 2010 concerning Minimum Service Standards for Integrated Services for Women and Children Victims of Violence;
30. Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 53 of 2011 concerning the Formulation of Regional Legal Products (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 694);
31. Regional Regulation of Trenggalek Regency Number 2 of 2009 concerning Principles of Regional Financial Management (Regional Gazette of Trenggalek Regency of 2009 Number 1 Series E);
32. Regional Regulation of Trenggalek Regency Number 22 of 2011 concerning Organization and Work Procedures of Regional Apparatus of Trenggalek Regency (Regional Gazette of Trenggalek Regency of 2011 Number 1 Series D);

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF TRENGGALEK REGENCY

and

THE REGENT OF TRENGGALEK

HAS DECIDED:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING THE IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR WOMEN AND CHILDREN.

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

1. The implementation of Protection of Women is based on Pancasila and the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia covering:
a. respect for human rights;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination; and
d. protection of victims.
2. The implementation of child protection is based on Pancasila and the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia as well as the basic principles of the Convention on the Rights of the Child covering:
a. non-discrimination;
b. the best interests of the child;
c. the right to life, survival, and development; and
d. respect for the views of the child.

Article 5

Every Woman has the right:
a. to live and to maintain her life and livelihood;
b. to form a family and continue descendants through lawful marriage;
c. to develop herself through the fulfillment of her basic needs;
d. to obtain education and benefit from science and technology, arts and culture, for the purpose of improving the quality of her life and for the welfare of humankind;
e. to advance herself in fighting for her rights collectively to build society, nation and state;
f. to recognition, guarantee, protection and just legal certainty as well as equal treatment before the law;
g. to work and receive fair and proper remuneration and treatment in employment relations;
h. to obtain equal opportunity in government;
i. to freedom of association, assembly and expression of opinion;
j. to protection of her person, family, honor, dignity and property under her control, and the right to a sense of security and protection from threats of fear to do or not do something which is a basic right;
k. to be free from torture or treatment that degrades human dignity and the right to obtain political asylum from another country;
l. to live in physical and spiritual prosperity, to reside and obtain a good and healthy living environment, and the right to obtain health services;
m. to social security that enables her full development as a dignified human being;
n. to equality of position in law and government;
o. to work and a livelihood proper for humanity;
p. in efforts to defend the state;
q. to communicate and obtain information to develop her personal and social environment, and the right to seek, obtain, possess, store, process, and convey information using all types of available channels;
r. to embrace her respective religion and to worship according to her religion and belief; and
s. to obtain education.

Article 8

1. To implement the provisions as referred to in Article 7, the Regional Government shall organize gender mainstreaming including:
a. establishing the implementation of gender mainstreaming;
b. coordinating, facilitating, and mediating gender mainstreaming;
c. facilitating institutional strengthening and development of gender mainstreaming mechanisms in government institutions, women's study centers, research and development institutions, non-governmental institutions;
d. implementing gender mainstreaming related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;
e. improving the quality of life of women related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;
f. integrating efforts to improve the quality of life of women related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;
g. coordinating the implementation of improving the quality of life of women related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;
h. organizing protection of women especially protection for victims of violence, female workers, elderly women, and women with disabilities, in conflict areas and disaster-affected areas;
i. facilitating the integration of protection of women especially protection for victims of violence, female workers, elderly women and women with disabilities, in conflict areas and disaster-affected areas;
j. coordinating protection of women especially protection for victims of violence, female workers, elderly women and women with disabilities;
k. facilitating the strengthening and development of networks of community institutions or organizations and the business sector for the implementation of gender mainstreaming;
l. developing a gender-based information system; and
m. providing and organizing integrated or non-integrated services for women victims of violence, female workers, elderly women, and women with disabilities.
2. Further provisions regarding forms of protection of women and gender mainstreaming mechanisms as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 10

Every child has the right:
a. to survival, growth, development and participation in a reasonable manner in accordance with human dignity and to receive protection from violence and discrimination;
b. to a name as personal identity and citizenship status;
c. to worship according to his/her religion, to think, and to express in accordance with his/her level of intelligence and age, under parental guidance;
d. to know his/her parents, to be raised, and cared for by his/her own parents;
e. to obtain health services and social security in accordance with physical, mental, spiritual, and social needs;
f. to obtain education and teaching in the context of developing his/her personality and level of intelligence in accordance with his/her interests and talents;
g. to express and be heard in his/her opinion, to receive, seek, and provide information in accordance with his/her level of intelligence and age for his/her self-development in accordance with the values of decency and propriety;
h. to rest and utilize leisure time, to associate with peers, to play, to recreate, and to create in accordance with his/her interests, talents, and level of intelligence for self-development;
i. to receive protection from discriminatory treatment, exploitation, whether economic or sexual, neglect, violence, abuse, injustice, other mistreatment, while in the care of parents, guardians, or any other party responsible for care;
j. to be cared for by his/her own parents, unless there are reasons and/or valid legal rules showing that such separation is in the best interests of the child and is a last resort; and
k. to obtain protection from abuse in political activities, involvement in armed conflict, involvement in social unrest, involvement in events containing elements of violence and involvement in warfare.

Article 16

Every child is obliged to:
a. respect parents, guardians, and teachers;
b. love family, community, and care for friends;
c. love the homeland, nation and state;
d. perform worship in accordance with the teachings of their religion; and
e. implement ethics and noble morals.

Article 19

1. To implement the provisions as referred to in Article 18, Regional Government organizes child rights mainstreaming including:
a. establishing the implementation of child rights mainstreaming;
b. coordinating and facilitating child rights mainstreaming;
c. facilitating institutional strengthening and development of child mainstreaming mechanisms in government institutions, research and development institutions, non-governmental institutions;
d. coordinating and facilitating child-responsive programs and activities and development of communication, information, and education about child rights mainstreaming;
e. implementing child rights mainstreaming related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;
f. improving the quality of life of children related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;
g. integrating development efforts related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;
h. coordinating the implementation of improving the quality of life of children related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;
i. seeking child protection especially for victims of violence, children with disabilities in conflict areas and disaster-affected areas;
j. facilitating the integration of child protection especially protection for victims of violence, children with disabilities, in conflict areas and disaster-affected areas;
k. coordinating child protection especially protection for victims of violence, children with disabilities, in conflict areas and disaster-affected areas;
l. facilitating the strengthening and development of networks of community institutions or organizations and the business world for the implementation of child rights mainstreaming; and
m. providing and organizing integrated or non-integrated services for child victims of violence, children with disabilities, and neglected children.
2. Further provisions regarding forms of child protection and mechanisms for mainstreaming children's rights as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 35

Education as referred to in Article 33 is directed at:
a. development of attitudes and personality abilities of children, talents, mental and physical abilities to achieve their optimal potential;
b. development of respect for human rights and fundamental freedoms;
c. development of respect for parents, cultural identity, language and their own values, national values where the child resides, from where the child originates, and civilizations that are different from one's own civilization;
d. preparation of children for a responsible life; and
e. development of respect and love for the environment.

Article 41

1. Regional Governments in organizing maintenance and care are obliged to strive for and assist children, so that children can:
a. participate;
b. freely express opinions and think in accordance with their conscience and religion;
c. freely receive oral or written information in accordance with the age stage and development of the child;
d. freely associate and assemble;
e. freely rest, play, recreate, create, and engage in cultural arts; and
f. obtain play facilities that meet health and safety requirements.
2. Efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are developed and adjusted to the age, level of ability of the child, and their environment so as not to hinder and disturb the development of the child.

Article 45

Children in emergency situations as referred to in Article 44 consist of:
a. children who become refugees;
b. children who are victims of riots;
c. children who are victims of natural disasters; and
d. children in armed conflict situations.

Article 49

1. Special protection for children in conflict with the law as referred to in Article 44 includes children in conflict with the law and child victims of criminal acts, and is the obligation and responsibility of the Regional Government and the community.
2. Special protection for children in conflict with the law as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented through:
a. treatment of children in a humane manner in accordance with the dignity and rights of the child;
b. provision of special child assistance officers from an early stage;
c. provision of special facilities and infrastructure;
d. imposition of appropriate sanctions for the best interests of the child;
e. continuous monitoring and recording of the development of children in conflict with the law;
f. provision of guarantees to maintain relationships with parents or family; and
g. protection from disclosure of identity through mass media and to avoid labeling.
3. Special protection for children who are victims of criminal acts as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented through:
a. rehabilitation efforts, both within institutions and outside institutions;
b. efforts to protect from disclosure of identity through mass media and to avoid labeling;
c. provision of safety guarantees for victim witnesses and expert witnesses, whether physical, mental, or social; and
d. provision of accessibility to obtain information regarding case developments.

Article 59

Every victim has the right to:
a. obtain protection from the family, Regional Government, police, prosecutor's office, court, advocates, social institutions or other parties either temporarily or based on a protection order from the Court;
b. obtain health services in accordance with medical needs;
c. obtain special handling related to victim confidentiality;
d. obtain assistance from social workers and legal aid at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations; and
e. obtain spiritual guidance services;
f. obtain protection from threats that endanger oneself, life and/or property whether before, during or after the case examination process from the Police;
g. obtain health rehabilitation, social rehabilitation, repatriation and social reintegration if the person concerned experiences suffering both physically and psychologically;
h. obtain restitution for victims of human trafficking crimes or their heirs in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations; and
i. other rights regulated in the provisions of Laws and Regulations.

Article 63

1. To implement the provisions as referred to in Article 62, the Regional Government:
a. formulates policies on efforts to prevent violence and prevent criminal acts of trafficking in persons against women and children;
b. formulates policies on the elimination of violence;
c. conducts communication, information, education, socialization, and advocacy regarding violence against women and children as well as criminal acts of trafficking in persons against women and children;
d. establishes institutions as a forum for cooperation in the protection and recovery of victims;
e. implements mechanisms for medical services, medicolegal services, psychosocial services, legal services, assistance services, spiritual guidance services, and victim recovery services;
f. provides infrastructure and facilities for the protection and recovery of victims;
g. facilitates the resocialization of victims, so that victims can resume their social functions in society; and
h. provides health rehabilitation services, social rehabilitation, repatriation, and social reintegration for victims of criminal acts of trafficking in persons against women and children.
2. Further provisions regarding the forms and mechanisms of victim protection as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.
